# Remote compute: where the CFD solves run. The choice lives in Preferences > Compute; Analyze > CFD table has the one-line
# `Runs on:` indicator with a Change… button that opens it. Run headless with a scratch settings file and the scripted solves
# (the real remote solve takes minutes and the driver waits for running tasks; `remote-setup` is a solve still building its worker
# on the remote, `remote-mid` one running on the remote GPU):
#   BOWSHOCK_SETTINGS_FILE=/tmp/remote-compute-settings.json BOWSHOCK_CFD_DEMO=remote-setup,remote-mid \
#     cargo run --release -q -p bowshock -- drive --example crates/bowshock/scripts/remote-compute.drive out/remote-compute
# `Test connection` talks to the real host below over SSH (key auth; the first run provisions the worker there).
viewport 1300x800
click "Analyze"

# Local: the CFD table method's indicator reads `Runs on: This computer`.
click "CFD table"
move (100, 700)
move (102, 702)
screenshot compute-1-local-analyze.png

# Preferences > Compute, This computer.
click "Change…"
screenshot compute-2-prefs-local.png

# Remote GPU with the target typed in.
click "Remote GPU"
click "user@host"
type "sandvoxel@192.168.1.88"
screenshot compute-3-prefs-remote.png

# Test connection against the real host: the set-up steps, then `<gpu> on <host>`.
click "Test connection"
screenshot compute-4-prefs-tested.png

# Back on Analyze: the first scripted solve (still building the worker on the remote) is cancelled and the second, `remote-mid`,
# runs on the remote GPU: the device line reads `<gpu> on <host>`.
click "Done"
click "Cancel"
click "Re-solve"
move (100, 700)
move (102, 702)
screenshot compute-5-remote-solve.png
